      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, generally, skin cancer presents similarly across different skin colors, including dark-skinned individuals. It often manifests as a new or changing skin growth, which can be noticed as a lump, a change in size, color, or elevation, or as a sore that doesn't heal. Clinicians might pay special attention to areas that typically show less visible changes in dark-skinned individuals, like the palms, soles, or underneath the nails. Regular skin checks and early medical assessments are crucial for all, regardless of skin color, due to potential differences in the appearance of an already developed skin cancer.",
